---
import Base from '../../../layouts/Base.astro';
import { specification, url } from '../../../data/site';
---

<Base
  title="How ANS works"
  description="Understand ANS registration, DNS discovery, transparency logs, and peer authentication through mTLS or DPoP."
>
  <section class="container page-head">
    <div class="grid page-head-grid">
      <h1 class="c-1-7">From domain name to verifiable peer</h1>
      <p class="lead c-8-12">
        ANS combines domain control, certificates, and a transparency log. Each
        part answers a specific question about the agent you are about to
        interact with.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <hr class="page-rule container" />

  <section class="section section--s">
    <div class="container grid">
      <div class="c-1-5">
        <h2>Register, publish, discover, verify</h2>
        <p class="intro-p">
          The agent operator, Registration Authority, and Transparency Log have
          distinct roles. DNS helps peers find the evidence.
        </p>
        <div class="callout">
          <strong>A name for a specific registration</strong>
          <code>ans://v1.0.0.payments.example.com</code>
          <p>
            The version and domain identify the registration. A new version is a
            new registration.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="c-6-12">
        <div class="sheet sheet--steps">
          <ol class="steps">
            <li>
              <div>
                <h3>Prove control and register</h3>
                <p>
                  The operator submits an agent host, version, endpoints, and
                  certificate material to a Registration Authority. The RA
                  verifies domain control and the required proofs.
                </p>
              </div>
            </li>
            <li>
              <div>
                <h3>Publish the required DNS records</h3>
                <p>
                  The RA returns the records to publish. The operator provisions
                  them with their DNS provider; the RA checks the required
                  records before activation.
                </p>
              </div>
            </li>
            <li>
              <div>
                <h3>Seal the lifecycle event</h3>
                <p>
                  The RA signs the event and the Transparency Log seals it
                  before activation succeeds. Public verification artifacts bind
                  the registration to its authorized certificates.
                </p>
              </div>
            </li>
            <li>
              <div>
                <h3>Discover the endpoint and evidence</h3>
                <p>
                  Peers use a supported discovery profile to locate endpoints.
                  The shared <code>_ans-badge</code> record locates the
                  registration’s badge in its Transparency Log.
                </p>
              </div>
            </li>
            <li>
              <div>
                <h3>Authenticate, then apply policy</h3>
                <p>
                  Check identity, current status, and key possession. The
                  application then applies its own permissions and risk policy
                  before allowing an action.
                </p>
              </div>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--s">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>Three proofs, one authenticated identity</h2>
      <div class="sheet proofs-sheet">
        <div
          class="table-wrap"
          tabindex="0"
          role="region"
          aria-label="Three proofs"
        >
          <table>
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th scope="col">Proof</th>
                <th scope="col">Question</th>
                <th scope="col">Evidence</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">1. Identity</th>
                <td data-label="Question">
                  Is this certificate bound to this agent in the transparency
                  log?
                </td>
                <td data-label="Evidence">
                  A badge or a verified SCITT receipt supplies the identity
                  evidence.
                </td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">2. Current status</th>
                <td data-label="Question">Is the registration still valid?</td>
                <td data-label="Evidence">
                  An online badge or a short-lived signed status token supplies
                  lifecycle information with bounded freshness.
                </td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">3. Possession</th>
                <td data-label="Question">
                  Does this peer hold the private key?
                </td>
                <td data-label="Evidence">
                  Public badges and receipts alone cannot authenticate the
                  caller. The peer must prove possession.
                </td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--l">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>Choose the proof that fits your connection</h2>
      <p class="intro-p">
        ANS-6 defines two caller authentication methods with equal standing in
        the published specification. Both require identity and current-status
        checks.
      </p>
      <div class="methods">
        <article>
          <h3>Method A, mutual TLS</h3>
          <p>
            The caller presents its ANS Identity Certificate during the TLS
            handshake. This fits a connection where the peer’s TLS identity
            reaches the verifier.
          </p>
          <p>Can use online badge verification or SCITT artifacts.</p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <h3>Method B, application-layer DPoP</h3>
          <p>
            The caller sends a signed, single-use proof bound to the request
            method, URL, and content. It can pass through gateways that
            terminate TLS.
          </p>
          <p>Uses a SCITT receipt and status token, with replay protection.</p>
        </article>
      </div>
      <p class="note">
        Callee verification also binds the actual TLS server certificate to the
        registration.{' '}
        <a
          href={`${specification}/blob/main/spec/ans-6-agent-authentication.md`}
        >
          Read the full ANS-6 verification procedures
        </a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--s">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>DNS is discovery, with verifiable bindings</h2>
      <p class="intro-p">
        The current V2 registration model defaults to <code>ANS_DNSAID</code>.{' '}
        <code>ANS_TXT</code> is an explicit option. Publish the records returned
        by your RA, including their required flags.
      </p>
      <div class="dns-table">
        <div class="sheet">
          <div
            class="table-wrap"
            tabindex="0"
            role="region"
            aria-label="DNS records"
          >
            <table>
              <caption>Published discovery profiles</caption>
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="col">Record</th>
                  <th scope="col">Location</th>
                  <th scope="col">Purpose</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="row" class="mono-cell">
                    ANS_DNSAID
                  </th>
                  <td data-label="Location">
                    SVCB at <code>agentHost</code>
                  </td>
                  <td data-label="Purpose">
                    Per-endpoint discovery hints. The default V2 profile.
                  </td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="row" class="mono-cell">
                    ANS_TXT
                  </th>
                  <td data-label="Location">
                    <code>_ans.agentHost</code>
                  </td>
                  <td data-label="Purpose">
                    TXT discovery records; opt-in on V2 and used by the V1 lane.
                  </td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="row">Badge</th>
                  <td data-label="Location">
                    <code>_ans-badge.agentHost</code>
                  </td>
                  <td data-label="Purpose">
                    Required, shared link to the Transparency Log badge.
                  </td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="row">Server DANE</th>
                  <td data-label="Location">
                    <code>_port._tcp.agentHost</code>
                  </td>
                  <td data-label="Purpose">
                    Optional TLSA record. A DNSSEC-authenticated mismatch must
                    not be accepted.
                  </td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="note">
        <a href={`${specification}/blob/main/spec/ans-3-dns-publication.md`}>
          DNS publication and profile details
        </a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container grid ruled prose-split">
      <h2 class="c-1-4">Trust boundaries: evidence has a scope</h2>
      <div class="c-5-12">
        <p>
          ANS verifies domain-anchored agent identity. Optional Verified
          Identities can add a separately proven identity relationship where the
          RA supports them.
        </p>
        <p>
          Sealed metadata digests can bind published metadata to a registration.
          They do not attest the code executing on a remote machine or guarantee
          an agent’s behavior.
        </p>
        <p>
          Transparency makes records auditable. Clients still need trusted log
          keys, correct signature and proof verification, freshness checks, and
          a policy for failures.
        </p>
        <p>
          ANS authentication does not authorize an action or delegate a user’s
          permissions. Trust scoring is a separate project, not an ANS
          authentication tier.
        </p>
        <a class="link-action" href={url('docs/')}>
          Read the specifications
        </a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
